t story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.61\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.95) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.42 to +1.09). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none.","t_rain":"Baton Rouge has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Baton Rouge's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.74 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +1.34), about +9.7 inches since 1894. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 61.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 2016 (90.54 in); the driest, 1924 (37.78 in). Baton Rouge almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.3 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}